{"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/swiftgodot\/multiplayerpeer\/transferchannel"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/MultiplayerPeer\/transferChannel","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"metadata":{"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"var"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"kind":"identifier","text":"transferChannel"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Int32","preciseIdentifier":"s:s5Int32V"}],"role":"symbol","externalID":"s:10SwiftGodot15MultiplayerPeerC15transferChannels5Int32Vvp","symbolKind":"property","roleHeading":"Instance Property","title":"transferChannel","modules":[{"name":"SwiftGodot"}]},"sections":[],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot","doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/MultiplayerPeer"]]},"abstract":[{"text":"The channel to use to send packets. Many network APIs such as ENet and WebRTC allow the creation of multiple independent channels which behaves, in a way, like separate connections. This means that reliable data will only block delivery of other packets on that channel, and ordering will only be in respect to the channel the packet is being sent on. Using different channels to send ","type":"text"},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"different and independent"}]},{"type":"text","text":" state updates is a common way to optimize network usage and decrease latency in fast-paced games."}],"kind":"symbol","primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"tokens":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"final"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"var"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"text":"transferChannel","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Int32","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:s5Int32V"},{"kind":"text","text":" { "},{"text":"get","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"kind":"keyword","text":"set"},{"text":" }","kind":"text"}],"languages":["swift"],"platforms":["macOS"]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"level":2,"anchor":"discussion","type":"heading","text":"Discussion"},{"style":"note","name":"Note","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The default channel (","type":"text"},{"code":"0","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":") actually works as 3 separate channels (one for each ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/MultiplayerPeer\/TransferMode-swift.enum"},{"text":") so that ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/MultiplayerPeer\/TransferMode-swift.enum\/reliable","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/MultiplayerPeer\/TransferMode-swift.enum\/unreliableOrdered","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" does not interact with each other by default. Refer to the specific network API documentation (e.g. ENet or WebRTC) to learn how to set up channels correctly."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"type":"aside"}]}],"references":{"doc://SwiftGodot/documentation/SwiftGodot/PacketPeer":{"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"class"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"PacketPeer","kind":"identifier"}],"kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftGodot\/documentation\/SwiftGodot\/PacketPeer","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"PacketPeer","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"text":"Abstraction and base class for packet-based protocols.","type":"text"}],"type":"topic","title":"PacketPeer","url":"\/documentation\/swiftgodot\/packetpeer","role":"symbol"},"doc://SwiftGodot/documentation/SwiftGodot/MultiplayerPeer/TransferMode-swift.enum":{"type":"topic","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"TransferMode","kind":"identifier"}],"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"enum"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"text":"TransferMode","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[],"kind":"symbol"